1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,840
Think of the moment you leave the shrine in
Breath of the Wild.

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:06,560
You take in the vast landscape, then move into
it yourself.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,560
Admiring a beautiful backdrop is different
from expecting to actually go there.

4
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:15,200
Today, let's look at what players should see
when a game begins.

5
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:20,200
Not the placement of menu buttons, but the
design of the first scene they can control.

6
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,780
That first scene makes a promise about the
experience ahead.

7
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:26,620
In an exploration game, it might be somewhere
you want to visit.

8
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,620
In a mystery, it might be a clue you want to
investigate.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,500
But when explanations line up before that
promise,

10
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,900
you arrive for an adventure and get employee
orientation instead.

11
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:39,620
What matters is not whether you explain
things, but what players experience first.

12
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:44,540
Let's compare two ways to open the same scene.
One explains the setting and

13
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:47,940
controls in order. The other first shows the
character and a nearby destination.

14
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,920
We are comparing opening priorities, not
features.

15
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:55,040
Either way, players should recognize what they
can expect from the game.

16
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:59,680
Before decorating the first screen, decide
what you want them to feel there.

17
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,960
Let's go back inside the Shrine of
Resurrection. Link wakes up,

18
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,060
obtains the Sheikah Slate, and opens the way
outside.

19
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,060
Voice guidance and control instructions appear
here too.

20
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:13,000
Breath of the Wild is not a game that explains
nothing.

21
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:18,920
What we are examining is how it presents an
immediate action, even in an unfamiliar space.

22
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,020
In the first controllable scene,

23
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:26,280
players need to read the relationship between
their character and the surroundings.

24
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:29,160
They first need to know where they are and
what looks worth approaching.

25
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:33,140
You can move toward a nearby object without
knowing the world's history.

26
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:36,560
Separating an understanding of the setting
from an understanding

27
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:39,360
of the first action reduces what the opening
demands all at once.

28
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:41,880
Our comparison keeps the character, exit,

29
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:46,480
and interactive object the same and changes
only the framing. We contrast an unclear

30
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:50,580
relationship with a composition that leads the
eye from character to object to exit.

31
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,460
Adding more arrows is not the only way to
guide someone. Can the positions within the

32
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:59,880
camera frame suggest the next action? That is
a design question for the first scene.

33
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:06,440
As you explore the Great Plateau, distant
terrain or buildings can become your next

34
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:10,500
point of interest. Before deciding where to
walk, notice what catches your eye.

35
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:14,760
Breath of the Wild also has quest guidance.
Separately from that guidance, let's examine

36
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:20,280
how something in the landscape can become a
destination you want to reach yourself.

37
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:24,220
A wide view does not always make direction
clear. When everything receives

38
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:29,500
similar emphasis, deciding where to look first
can become difficult.

39
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:34,800
Size, contrast, and surrounding empty space
can establish an order of attention.

40
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:39,320
Instead of revealing the entire route, choose
what will first catch the player's eye.

41
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:42,660
With the same terrain layout, compare a view
where several objects compete

42
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:46,220
for attention with one that distinguishes a
first point of interest.

43
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:49,800
We are not adding destinations or changing the
map.

44
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:53,480
If everything looks important, the whole
screen is raising its hand at once.

45
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:59,360
Designing the first scene also means deciding
what not to emphasize.

46
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:04,030
Let's look at noticing and picking up a nearby
item on the Plateau. Without starting

47
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:09,810
a grand battle, a small experience emerges:
looking around and finding something.

48
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:14,990
We are interested less in the item feature
itself than in this action's role in

49
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:18,330
the opening. It can suggest that this is a
world where looking around is welcome.

50
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:22,890
The first action is more than a button check.
It is a chance to communicate through

51
00:03:22,890 --> 00:03:27,510
play whether this is a game about observing,
avoiding danger, or making choices.

52
00:03:27,510 --> 00:03:31,890
Demanding several actions at once can obscure
what the player has learned.

53
00:03:32,270 --> 00:03:36,510
First decide which single action you want them
to experience in this scene.

54
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:40,750
Our comparison shows the same space with many
feature instructions at once,

55
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:43,850
then with one nearby object to investigate
first.

56
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:47,270
It illustrates how the scope of that first
attempt changes.

57
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:51,370
A simple beginning does not mean the whole
game must be simple.

58
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:55,070
The deeper the game, the more reason to ask
whether players must

59
00:03:55,070 --> 00:03:57,170
immediately be thrown in at the deep end.
Think about the order of entry.

60
00:03:57,950 --> 00:04:01,930
In the shrine where you obtain Magnesis, you
move metal plates to open a path.

61
00:04:02,110 --> 00:04:04,190
The game does not stop at telling you about a
new ability.

62
00:04:04,190 --> 00:04:07,330
It lets you see what using it changes.

63
00:04:07,870 --> 00:04:10,970
Do not watch only the moving object.

64
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:15,190
Also notice how that movement connects to the
route ahead.

65
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:19,210
After the first interaction, players need
evidence that it worked. If pressing a button

66
00:04:19,470 --> 00:04:25,390
seems to change nothing, they may press it
again instead of taking the next action.

67
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:27,930
Good feedback is not simply a flash of light.

68
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:30,990
The relationship between an action and a
change in the space should be visible,

69
00:04:30,990 --> 00:04:33,710
and that change should provide a reason to
continue.

70
00:04:34,210 --> 00:04:39,970
With the same button and door, compare an
off-screen result with a framing that shows

71
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:45,450
the door opening. This is about making the
result readable, not writing switch code.

72
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:50,270
If a game needs the result to happen
off-screen, sound or a brief camera

73
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:52,790
cue can help. Keeping everything in one frame
is not always the answer.

74
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:55,850
The key is making cause and effect read as
connected.

75
00:04:56,950 --> 00:05:01,510
As exploration continues on the Great Plateau,
new problems such as cold appear. Players

76
00:05:01,630 --> 00:05:06,670
examine temperature and status, then consider
responses such as clothing or food effects.

77
00:05:06,810 --> 00:05:11,830
This happens later than the first controllable
scene. It shows how learning

78
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:16,450
that began in the opening develops into an
experience when a new danger appears.

79
00:05:16,590 --> 00:05:22,570
An opening does not have to be safe. Some
games need a dangerous or unsettling

80
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:26,310
beginning. What matters is what the player can
understand after failing.

81
00:05:26,310 --> 00:05:29,230
A failure whose cause you recognize and can
respond to differs

82
00:05:29,230 --> 00:05:33,230
from being hurt for no apparent reason.
Separate from difficulty,

83
00:05:33,230 --> 00:05:36,210
examine the visual clues that explain the
danger.

84
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:39,010
For the same hazard, one version hides the
cause while

85
00:05:39,010 --> 00:05:43,270
the other connects the danger zone to a change
in status.

86
00:05:43,410 --> 00:05:47,130
We are not removing difficulty, but checking
whether its reason is readable.

87
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:52,350
If the route back to another attempt is too
long, early learning can become repetitive

88
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:56,690
labor. Design both the presentation of danger
and the flow back after failure.

89
00:05:57,450 --> 00:05:59,750
Breath of the Wild includes the Old Man's
guidance and dialogue,

90
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:02,550
as well as a goal for leaving the Great
Plateau. In ordinary progression,

91
00:06:02,810 --> 00:06:07,710
you complete four shrines, obtain the
paraglider, and head into the next region.

92
00:06:08,070 --> 00:06:13,090
So describing its opening as having no
explanations or restrictions

93
00:06:13,650 --> 00:06:16,310
misses something important. Guidance and
exploration coexist.

94
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:18,510
When choosing information for the first
screen, separate what players need now

95
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:22,950
from what they will need later. The problem
may not be incorrect information,

96
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:28,070
but too much information arriving before there
is a reason to use it.

97
00:06:28,410 --> 00:06:32,090
Conversely, some scenes become
incomprehensible without an explanation.

98
00:06:32,690 --> 00:06:34,430
Instead of simply reducing instructions,

99
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:37,910
ask whether they answer the player's current
question.

100
00:06:38,310 --> 00:06:41,910
Our comparison keeps the information unchanged
and alters only its timing.

101
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:44,270
We place a sequence that shows everything at
the start beside

102
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:47,830
one that offers relevant guidance after the
first action.

103
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:51,670
In a story-driven game, the opening
conversation may itself be the core

104
00:06:51,785 --> 00:06:54,370
experience. The distinction is not bad
cutscenes versus good interaction.

105
00:06:54,370 --> 00:06:58,710
Ask whether what you are showing begins this
game's experience.

106
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:05,050
Gliding beyond the Plateau lets you explore
the world you have been seeing in a new way.

107
00:07:05,370 --> 00:07:08,610
Actions from the opening area carry into the
adventure ahead.

108
00:07:09,030 --> 00:07:12,930
When evaluating an opening, do not stop at
whether its first screen looks good.

109
00:07:13,310 --> 00:07:17,650
Examine whether the expectation it creates
carries into the next scene.

110
00:07:17,770 --> 00:07:22,830
Ask three questions when designing the first
scene. What should players see first?

111
00:07:22,830 --> 00:07:27,590
What action should they try? Why should the
result make them want to continue?

112
00:07:27,590 --> 00:07:31,370
Treat the advice about the first three minutes
not as a timer for every game,

113
00:07:31,370 --> 00:07:35,350
but as a question about when the core
experience begins.

114
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:38,130
Rushing the presentation is not the point.

115
00:07:38,570 --> 00:07:41,770
Our final comparison contrasts a beginning
that stalls after the first goal

116
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:44,730
with one whose result reveals another point of
interest. Where you end the

117
00:07:45,190 --> 00:07:50,330
scene and what you leave behind can change the
reason for the next action.

118
00:07:50,750 --> 00:07:53,670
A game's first scene is not a manual that
explains everything.

119
00:07:53,670 --> 00:07:58,170
It is an invitation to try. Rather than
listing everything you want to teach,

120
00:07:58,170 --> 00:08:01,030
start by designing the experience you are
inviting players into.
